          "type": "turn",
          "question": "worst hedache ive ever had, came on like a bang while i was lifting, and the lights hurt",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now (999 or 112 in the UK and Europe, 911 in the US, or your local number).** Do not wait to see if the headache settles. Your symptoms match the guidance for a sudden, severe headache that reaches full strength within about a minute, which needs emergency assessment even if it has eased (*Sudden severe headache and other dangerous headache patterns*, emergency medicine and neurology guidance). Only a clinician can tell you what is causing it.\n\n**Red flags being considered:**\n- **Thunderclap headache:** a sudden headache at full strength within about a minute, often described as the worst of one's life. This is the main pattern in your description.\n- **Meningitis signs:** the guidance lists severe dislike of bright light alongside headache and fever. Check for fever, a stiff neck, confusion, drowsiness, or a rash of spots that does not fade when pressed (*Signs suggesting meningitis or meningococcal infection*).\n- **Stroke signs:** the guidance lists sudden severe headache with no known cause as a stroke warning. Check for face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech, sudden confusion, or vision loss (*Sudden neurological symptoms that may indicate a stroke*).\n\nThe passages I retrieved do not name subarachnoid haemorrhage (bleeding around the brain) specifically. The emergency team will decide what needs checking.\n\n**What to do now:**\n- Call emergency services. Do not drive yourself.\n- Stop lifting or any strenuous activity. Lie down somewhere quiet and dim.\n- Do not be alone. Ask someone to stay with you and to watch for confusion, drowsiness, or weakness.\n- Tell the call handler exactly when it started, that it came on suddenly while lifting, and about the light sensitivity. Also tell them about any fever, stiff neck, vomiting, or other symptoms.\n- Tell the crew about any medicines you have taken today.\n- If you become drowsy, confused, weak, or have a seizure, or if you lose consciousness, tell the crew straight away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
